# Guide: add a new endpoint

Walks through adding a `widgets` resource end-to-end, following the
same shape as the existing `items` resource — copy that shape for any
new resource rather than inventing a new one.

## 1. Define the schemas

In `src/{{ package_name }}/schemas.py`, add the request/response models:

```python
class WidgetCreate(BaseModel):
    name: str
    description: str | None = None


class WidgetRead(BaseModel):
    id: int
    name: str
    description: str | None = None
{% if orm == "sqlmodel" %}
    model_config = {"from_attributes": True}
{% elif orm == "sqlalchemy" %}
    model_config = {"from_attributes": True}
{% endif %}
```

{% if database != "none" %}
## 2. Define the model

In `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`, add the ORM model (mirror the
existing `Item` model's shape — same `{{ orm }}` conventions, see the
`{{ orm }}` skill for the model-definition patterns this project uses).

## 3. Write the router
{% else %}
## 2. Write the router
{% endif %}

Create `src/{{ package_name }}/routes/widgets.py`:

```python
from fastapi import APIRouter, HTTPException
{% if database != "none" %}
from fastapi import Depends

from {{ package_name }}.core.db import get_session
from {{ package_name }}.models import Widget
{% endif %}
from {{ package_name }}.schemas import WidgetCreate, WidgetRead

router = APIRouter(prefix="/widgets", tags=["widgets"])


@router.get("/", response_model=list[WidgetRead])
{% if database != "none" %}
async def list_widgets(session=Depends(get_session)) -> list[Widget]:
    ...  # same query shape as routes/items.py's list_items
{% else %}
def list_widgets() -> list[WidgetRead]:
    ...  # same in-memory-store shape as routes/items.py
{% endif %}
```

Follow `routes/items.py` for the rest of the CRUD handlers (`POST`,
`GET /{id}`, `DELETE /{id}`) — same status codes (`201` on create,
`204` on delete, `404` via `HTTPException` when not found), same
`response_model=` on every handler.

